WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.060
Why can a cut limb nerve regrow when spinal axons mostly stall?

2
00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.560
And why can nerve injury turn light touch into lasting pain?

3
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:13.340
To find out, labs cut or crush nerves and track what follows.

4
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.940
Cutting an axon, called axotomy, or a severe crush splits it in two.

5
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:26.260
The proximal piece stays joined to the cell body; the distal piece separates.

6
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:31.540
In mouse sciatic nerve, it looks normal for a day and a half to two days.

7
00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.140
After that latent phase, it breaks apart.

8
00:00:36.183 --> 00:00:41.323
In a peripheral nerve, debris clears over days to weeks, and the proximal tip may

9
00:00:41.323 --> 00:00:46.163
regrow. Human regrowth often runs around a millimetre a day.

10
00:00:47.463 --> 00:00:52.563
In the spinal cord, distal axons break down too, yet clearing is slower and

11
00:00:52.563 --> 00:00:56.063
incomplete. Many central axons stall.

12
00:00:57.303 --> 00:00:59.703
Is the fault in the neurons, or their surroundings?

13
00:01:01.563 --> 00:01:05.483
Controlled nerve injury separates breakdown, regrowth, and rewired pain.

14
00:01:06.567 --> 00:01:09.787
For lasting pain, labs use spared nerve injury.

15
00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:13.987
Two branches to a rat's paw are cut; one is spared.

16
00:01:15.387 --> 00:01:18.467
Calibrated von Frey filaments press up through wire mesh.

17
00:01:19.767 --> 00:01:24.727
Many labs take the force lifting the paw half the time as the withdrawal threshold.

18
00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:31.467
Days later, it drops: the rodent sign of allodynia, pain from normally gentle touch.

19
00:01:32.517 --> 00:01:36.437
After injury, sensory neurons can fire with no stimulus.

20
00:01:37.817 --> 00:01:42.497
In one model, the spinal cord's sensory entry zone, the dorsal horn, loses

21
00:01:42.497 --> 00:01:47.137
inhibition. Then a light touch can fire a pain-signalling neuron.

22
00:01:48.397 --> 00:01:51.117
Pause and think: what can that paw lift not tell you?

23
00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:00.290
A rat cannot say a touch hurts, so a paw lift is a stand-in.

24
00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:06.370
Separate brain circuits set unpleasantness, and spontaneous pain is hard to measure.

25
00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:12.450
Recovery over time may reflect which neurons survived, so labs trace injured

26
00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:18.670
neurons. Routes differ with sex and injury, and injury mechanisms may not act in

27
00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:19.670
disease.

28
00:02:20.167 --> 00:02:26.207
First, in 2020, Renthal and colleagues cut or crushed adult mouse nerves, then

29
00:02:26.207 --> 00:02:29.087
sequenced single nuclei from sensory ganglia.

30
00:02:30.507 --> 00:02:36.147
Injury suppressed the marker genes of neuron subtypes, and Atf3 was required for

31
00:02:36.147 --> 00:02:37.207
full reprogramming.

32
00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:42.430
Identity re-emerged on a time course associated with regrowth.

33
00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:49.470
An Atf3 reporter's share held from one week to two months, arguing against selective

34
00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:50.470
death.

35
00:02:50.883 --> 00:02:57.543
In 2015, Peirs and colleagues deleted VGLUT3 from mouse dorsal-horn neurons.

36
00:02:58.803 --> 00:03:03.443
Withdrawal thresholds rose after nerve injury, so stronger touch was needed.

37
00:03:04.723 --> 00:03:10.783
Also in 2015, Petitjean and colleagues removed spinal parvalbumin neurons in male

38
00:03:10.783 --> 00:03:17.783
mice. That caused mechanical allodynia, linked to lifting inhibition on PKC-gamma

39
00:03:17.783 --> 00:03:18.783
interneurons.

40
00:03:19.533 --> 00:03:25.053
Activating parvalbumin neurons eased nerve-injury-associated hypersensitivity.

41
00:03:26.453 --> 00:03:32.333
Whether an axon regrows, stalls, or rewires touch into pain, a controlled break

42
00:03:32.333 --> 00:03:33.913
makes the mechanism testable.
